## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `ProductCatalogTests.cs`

### Summary

**10 tests reviewed · 6 Critical · 1 High · 1 Medium · 1 Low**

This suite is a textbook **coverage-touching** anti-pattern. Every public method on `ProductCatalog` is called, achieving high line coverage, but **6 of 10 tests have zero meaningful assertions**. The remaining 4 assert only `IsNotNull`, which is nearly as weak. This explains why bugs reach production — the tests prove code *runs* without crashing, not that it *works correctly*.

---

### Critical Findings

#### 1. Systematic Coverage-Touching (suite-wide)

The entire suite methodically calls every public method in declaration order with no real verification. This is the root cause of your "95% coverage, still buggy" problem.

#### 2. No Assertions — 6 tests (lines 17–21, 33–36, 39–42, 63–66, 69–74, 85–88)

| Test | What it misses |
|---|---|
| `AddProduct_Works` | Never verifies the product was actually stored |
| `UpdatePrice_Works` | Never checks the price changed to 14.99 |
| `RemoveProduct_Works` | Never checks the product is gone |
| `GetProductCount_Works` | Never checks the count value |
| `ApplyBulkDiscount_Works` | Never checks prices were reduced |
| `ImportFromCsv_Works` | Never checks a product was imported |

**Fix example — `UpdatePrice_Works` (line 33):**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void UpdatePrice_UpdatesStoredPrice()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    _catalog.UpdatePrice("SKU-001", 14.99m);
    var updated = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.AreEqual(14.99m, updated!.Price);
}
```

**Fix example — `RemoveProduct_Works` (line 39):**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void RemoveProduct_ProductNoLongerRetrievable()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    _catalog.RemoveProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.IsNull(_catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001"));
}
```

#### 3. Trivial `IsNotNull` Assertions — 4 tests (lines 28, 51, 59, 81)

`GetProduct`, `SearchProducts`, `GetAllProducts`, and `ExportToCsv` only check `IsNotNull`. An empty list or wrong product would pass fine.

**Fix example — `SearchProducts_Works` (line 46):**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void Search_MatchingQuery_ReturnsMatchingProducts()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
